(project pricklypear-habitat-workflow
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: habitat-workflow")
 (status implemented)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: libs/spec (~600 lines) + promote_soft_warnings in session.ml; live spec/todo+log/sessions+log/issues docs; /book/todo 200. Soft lint only (no hard bounce yet — intentional v1). /spec health 500 is runtime bug not missing package.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section habitat-workflow
  (doc "Spec-in-the-image: the borge discipline (spec -> build -> compare) as a habitat-native workflow, with promote as the goalpost-immobility gate")
  (status implemented)

  (verify
   (smoke "notes docs spec/<pkg> exist for at least one package and pass habitat lint queries")
   (smoke "redefining a flow-listed public binding without a spec/decisions mention shows up in the drift report"))

  (details
   (doc (|
        THE PROBLEM THIS CHAPTER EXISTS TO SOLVE

        Package development on the habitat is agent-driven and
        session-based (phone REPL, pp_eval, browser). Without an
        artifact that outlives the turn, agents exhibit three
        failure modes:

        1. Multiple happy paths through one workflow — nothing
           records which path was chosen, so each session
           re-derives "how this works" from the code, and code
           under-specifies that.
        2. Redundant problems — no registry of known problems,
           so the same issue is "discovered" twice and fixed two
           incompatible ways.
        3. Flip-flop rewrites — solving A with B's approach,
           breaking B, reimplementing A. Nothing records that the
           current shape was a DECISION WITH A REASON, so
           broken-looking code reads as "never wanted" instead of
           "wanted, currently broken."

        File-borge suppresses these by forcing intent to exist as
        an artifact (the .borg book) that outlives the turn, and
        by gating edits so the agent cannot move the target while
        shooting at it. This chapter ports the DISCIPLINE, not the
        file mechanics.

        THE CENTRAL MOVE: GATE THE PROMOTE, NOT THE REPL

        File-borge gates keystrokes (plan/make file locks) because
        a working tree has no cheaper boundary. The habitat has a
        much better boundary already: the branch lens. So
        habitat-workflow gates the PROMOTE. Pre-commit hooks vs CI.

        - The REPL stays playful and chaotic. Agents may thrash
          freely on user/personal and user/dev branches.
        - Nothing reaches system/main unless the comparison loop
          passes (spec-checks).
        - Promote (branching.promote) becomes pp's commit, and it
          grows a checklist. Review becomes looking at a page, not
          running a toolchain.

        THE LITERATE INVERSION

        In file-borge the book and the code live in different
        formats in different files, so a whole tool (balance /
        drift / report) exists to correlate them. On the habitat,
        spec docs, function docs, and code are ALL sexps in the
        same store under the same branching. So the book stops
        being a source artifact and becomes a VIEW: a spec doc's
        outline owns the narrative order; the referenced bindings
        fill in underneath via their function-doc and source.
        Drift stops being a report you run and becomes RENDERING:
        a chapter whose referenced binding was renamed shows a
        hole where the paragraph used to be. There is nothing to
        sync because the book route is a query.

        RELATIONSHIP TO THE FILE BOOK

        The OCaml kernel (image/, lisp/, migrations/) remains
        governed by THIS multi-file .borg book; git stays its
        source of truth. Habitat-workflow governs Nopales package
        code (libs/*, and their live counterparts on branches).
        Habitat spec docs are NOTES DOCS, so they branch like
        everything else: humans edit them on system/main; agents
        on branches propose changes via spec-issues and pending
        edits that the human accepts at promote time. The
        authorship-integrity rule file-borge enforces by lint
        ("agents never touch human comments") is here STRUCTURAL:
        spec docs live on main, agent sessions cannot write main,
        so the violation is impossible rather than detectable.
        pp-sync snapshot remains the durability/vault path for
        spec docs exactly as for package code.
   |))
  )

  (subsection spec-docs
   (doc "One notes doc per package (slug spec/<pkg>): flows, canonical paths, decisions, status — the intent artifact that outlives the turn")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: convention + tooling live — (notes/doc/get "spec/todo") #t; spec/doc, flow-entries, flows-resolve in libs/spec. Coverage across all packages is ongoing content, not missing mechanism.|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "(notes/doc/get \"spec/todo\") returns a doc with flows/decisions sections")
    (smoke "a spec doc's flow entries name existing function bindings (resolvable via function-doc)"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Every package that receives agent-driven development gets
         ONE notes doc with slug spec/<pkg>. It is short and
         structured, not prose-heavy:

         FLOWS
           The user-visible workflows, numbered. Each flow names
           the ONE canonical path through the bindings, e.g.
           "add -> todo/create -> todo/render-row". New code paths
           must attach to a flow or create one — this is what
           kills multiple-happy-paths.

         DECISIONS
           An append-mostly log of non-obvious choices WITH
           REASONS ("render is a pure function of the row so htmx
           swaps stay dumb"). This is the flip-flop killer: the
           next session can distinguish "wanted, currently broken"
           from "never wanted."

         STATUS
           One line per flow: works / half-built / broken.

         Spec docs live on system/main (shared intent). Branch
         lenses mean an agent CAN fork a spec doc onto its branch
         to draft a proposal, but promote of spec docs is a
         human-visible, human-approved step (see spec-checks).
         Function-level docs (define docstrings,
         lisp-interpreter.define-docstring) are the per-binding
         layer; the spec doc is the per-package narrative layer.
         A function-doc entry SHOULD reference its flow number(s).
   |))
  
   )
  )
  (subsection session-ritual
   (doc "The agent-facing protocol: branch-scoped work, obituary rule, file-don't-fix, handoff — recorded in the live agents note")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (smoke "(notes/doc/get \"agents\") contains the session-ritual rules (obituary, file-don't-fix, handoff)")
    (smoke "log/issues and log/sessions notes docs exist and accrete entries"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         The durable agent guide is the notes doc with slug
         agents (advertised in /agent.txt); habitat-workflow
         adds these rules to it. All are notes-doc mechanics,
         zero kernel work:

         1. WORK ON YOUR BRANCH. Agent sessions define/route on
            <user>/personal or a session dev branch, never
            directly on system/main. End of session: diff the
            lens (which bindings did I touch?), self-review
            against the declared flows, promote or revert.

         2. NO SILENT REVERSALS (the obituary rule). An agent
            may not replace or delete an existing implementation
            without first writing its obituary in the spec doc's
            decisions section: what it was, why it existed, what
            changed in the world that invalidates it. Writing the
            obituary forces the agent to notice it is flip-
            flopping; half the time it will realize the old
            approach was right and something ELSE is broken.

         3. FILE, DON'T FIX. Out-of-scope discoveries go to the
            log/issues doc, one line each, instead of expanding
            session scope. This is borge issue new for the
            habitat — and since notes docs are data, it needs no
            gitignored directory.

         4. HANDOFF NOTE. Session ends with ~3 lines appended to
            log/sessions: what changed, what is half-done, what
            was deliberately NOT done. Flip-flop mostly happens
            ACROSS sessions, when the next session cannot tell
            half-built from broken.

         Enforcement is by instruction (agents note) first;
         mechanical checks (spec-checks) make violations visible
         rather than relying on agent virtue.
   |))
  
   )
  )
  (subsection ritual-tooling
   (doc "Laptop-side scripts that make the session ritual cheap: file the issue, assert on eval JSON, recover a notes doc — operator/agent tooling living outside the image")
   (status implemented)
   (implements
    scripts/eval_json.sh
    scripts/file-issue.sh
    scripts/recover_agents_outline.py)
   (verify
    (smoke "scripts/file-issue.sh submits one line to the habitat log/issues register (opens the /issues face or POSTs notes/issues/add)")
    (smoke "sourcing scripts/eval_json.sh gives smoke scripts python3-json assertions over pp_eval -j / POST /api/eval responses instead of brittle grep -q '"ok":true'")
    (smoke "scripts/recover_agents_outline.py rebuilds a notes doc outline from latest tree_versions rows and writes it back via POST /api/eval (2026-09-04 agents-note incident recovery; node.toml one-off tool for doc-level recovery)"))
  )

  (subsection spec-checks
   (doc "Promote-time comparison: spec->code resolution, code->spec acknowledgment, doc coverage — promote grows a checklist")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: spec/lint + promote-checklist + Session.promote_soft_warnings wired into promote_to_main and branches UI (soft, non-blocking). Hard bounce graduation still future; matches "starting as soft warnings".|) *)
   (verify
    (smoke "promote preview lists flow bindings that fail to resolve on the source branch")
    (smoke "promote preview lists public bindings redefined on the source branch with no spec/decisions mention")
    (smoke "promote preview lists public bindings with empty function-doc"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Promote (branching.promote) is the commit of the
         habitat; this subsection hangs the comparison loop on
         it. Before upserting, promote (or its preview UI) runs
         three checks against the source branch and the package
         spec doc, and reports — starting as soft warnings,
         graduating to bounces for admin-into-main promotes:

         1. SPEC->CODE: every flow in spec/<pkg> names its
            canonical bindings; they MUST all resolve on the
            source branch. This is borge drift, but exact —
            sexp references, not grep.

         2. CODE->SPEC: every PUBLIC binding redefined on the
            source branch is mentioned somewhere in the spec
            doc or its decisions section. Redefining
            todo/render without a spec acknowledgment bounces.
            This is the anti-flip-flop tripwire: the mechanical
            form of the obituary rule.

         3. DOC COVERAGE: promote refuses undocumented PUBLIC
            bindings (empty function-doc). define stays loose —
            docs are not an ABI change, they are a shipping
            requirement. Undocumented is fine on your branch,
            unshippable on main. Docs get written at the moment
            of "this is real now," which is also the moment the
            agent can best explain what it built and why.

         "Public" = bindings referenced by routes or by other
         packages (the surface a consumer can name); internal
         helpers are exempt. SPEC DOC CHANGES travel through
         promote too, and are shown to the human explicitly
         ("this promotion also modifies spec/todo — accept?").
         Friction, not locks: one tap to approve; the agent can
         never silently ratchet the goalposts.

         DEPENDENCY NOTE: checks 2–3 want define's persist path
         to be synchronous enough that the check sees the write
         (the fire-and-forget persist race must be fixed first;
         .borge-bugs). A phase check that races the write is
         theater.
   |))
  
   )
  )
  (subsection book-view
   (doc "The literate inversion: spec doc outlines own narrative order; bindings inline underneath — drift becomes rendering")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (smoke "GET /book/todo renders the spec doc outline with referenced bindings' doc + source inlined")
    (smoke "a spec doc referencing a renamed binding renders a visible hole instead of stale prose"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         A route /book/<pkg> (private) renders the spec doc as a
         chapter: walk the outline; at each flow or binding
         reference, inline the binding's function-doc and source
         (both are sexps in the same store). The book is a query,
         so it is ALWAYS current — the thing Knuth wanted and
         files could never quite give.

         DRIFT AS RENDERING: a reference that no longer resolves
         renders as a visible hole in the page (styled, obvious),
         not as a line in a report. The book physically shows its
         own staleness.

         This complements, not replaces, the file book: the OCaml
         kernel's chapters remain .borg files. /book serves the
         Nopales package layer. Whether the two ever merge into
         one rendered volume is an open question, explicitly not
         decided here.
   |))
  
   )
  )
  (subsection spec-health
   (doc "Habitat health page: packages x (flows verified, doc coverage, drift holes) — borge report as a browser page")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (smoke "GET /spec lists every package with a spec doc, its flow resolution status, and doc coverage"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         /spec is the habitat's standing health page (private,
         admin): one row per package that has a spec doc —
         flows resolving / broken, doc coverage count, count of
         drift holes in /book rendering, last promote. This is
         borge report for the image, sized for a phone screen:
         review becomes LOOKING AT A PAGE.

         Discovery contract: describe / apropos-docs surface doc
         coverage alongside results ("todo: 14 bindings, 3
         undocumented"), so the gap follows the agent around
         instead of waiting for a report run.
   |))
  
   )
  )
  (subsection working-docs
   (doc "Session working docs under docs/: make-phase plans, design proposals, review findings, handoffs — the ralph-loop shared memory that complements the spec docs")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|These are POINT-IN-TIME working artifacts, not spec: plan files (docs/plans/*) drive ralph/loop make phases and are the shared memory between loops; reviews (security-review-2026-09, theme-hex-audit) and redesign proposals (chat-ui-redesign, component-ui-proposal) record findings + drafts whose accepted content gets promoted into .borg chapters; treansversal handoffs (tree-traversal-handoff) and cutover records (one-tree-userland-cutover) capture state between sessions. Where a doc's content is ACCEPTED SPEC, the owning .borg chapter is the authority and the doc is evidence/trail. cs-curriculum is the curriculum plan behind course/cs-interpreters.borg (habitat-book.course-documents).|) *)
   (implements
    docs/chat-ui-redesign.md
    docs/component-ui-proposal.md
    docs/cs-curriculum.md
    docs/one-tree-userland-cutover.md
    docs/plans/delete-lwt-rim.md
    docs/plans/filmstrip-v1.md
    docs/plans/soil-m-a.md
    docs/plans/soil-m-b.md
    docs/plans/window-controlbox-v2.md
    docs/plans/window-controlbox-v3.md
    docs/security-review-2026-09.md
    docs/theme-hex-audit.md
    docs/tree-traversal-handoff.md)
  )

  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "What habitat-workflow is NOT")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: documentation-only; marked implemented as spec-complete.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         - NOT a port of borge-the-tool into Nopales. No balance,
           no parse, no lint pipeline; the store is the parser.
         - NOT kernel governance. OCaml/migrations stay under
           the file book and git, full stop.
         - NOT REPL gating. No plan/make modes on eval, no
           dynamic-extent phase locks on define. Play is
           unrestricted on branches; the gate is promote.
         - NOT a rewrite of branching.promote's data model.
           This chapter adds checks around the existing
           selective-upsert mechanism.
         - NOT mandatory for every package. One-off garden
           scripts and personal-branch experiments never need a
           spec doc. Spec docs are for packages with multiple
           agent sessions of history — exactly where the three
           failure modes bite.
         - NOT provenance/audit of AI-authored code — that is
           ai-authoring.provenance. This chapter consumes
           author info but defines no new attribution model.
   |))
  
 

   )
  )
 )
)
